
## 🎯 Project: "Auto-Scaling Web App with Load Balancer"

### What You'll Build:
1. **Launch Template** (Blueprint for EC2 instances)
2. **Auto Scaling Group** (ASG) with scaling policies
3. **Application Load Balancer** (ALB) to distribute traffic
4. **Everything works together** seamlessly

---


## 📝 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1: Create a Launch Template

**AWS Console: **
1. Go to **EC2 → Launch Templates** → **Create launch template**
2. Fill in:

| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Name** | `WebApp-LT` |
| **AMI** | Amazon Linux 2 (latest) |
| **Instance Type** | `t2.micro` |
| **Key Pair** | Select your existing key |
| **Security Group** | Create new or select one allowing **HTTP (80)** from anywhere |
| **User Data** | Paste the script below |

**User Data Script:**
```bash
#!/bin/bash
yum update -y
yum install -y httpd

# Get instance metadata
INSTANCE_ID=$(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id)
AZ=$(curl -s http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/availability-zone)

# Create web page
cat <<EOF > /var/www/html/index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head><title>Load Balancer Test</title></head>
<body style="font-family: Arial; text-align: center; padding: 50px;">
    <h1 style="color: #ff9900;">🚀 Auto Scaling Demo</h1>
    <p><strong>Instance ID:</strong> $INSTANCE_ID</p>
    <p><strong>Availability Zone:</strong> $AZ</p>
    <p><strong>Current Time:</strong> $(date)</p>
    <hr>
    <p>✅ This instance is part of an Auto Scaling Group!</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

systemctl start httpd
systemctl enable httpd
```

---

### Step 2: Create Target Group

**AWS Console:**
1. Go to **EC2 → Target Groups** → **Create target group**
2. Fill in:

| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Target type** | Instances |
| **Name** | `WebApp-TG` |
| **Protocol** | HTTP |
| **Port** | 80 |
| **VPC** | Select your default VPC |
| **Health check path** | `/` |
| **Advanced settings** | |
| - **Healthy threshold** | 2 |
| - **Unhealthy threshold** | 2 |
| - **Timeout** | 5 seconds |
| - **Interval** | 30 seconds |

3. Click **Create**

---

### Step 3: Create Load Balancer

**AWS Console:**
1. Go to **EC2 → Load Balancers** → **Create load balancer**
2. Choose **Application Load Balancer**

**Basic Configuration:**
| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Name** | `WebApp-ALB` |
| **Scheme** | Internet-facing |
| **IP address type** | IPv4 |

**Network Mapping:**
| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **VPC** | Select default VPC |
| **Availability Zones** | Select **at least 2 AZs** |

**Security Groups:**
- Select or create a SG allowing:
  - **HTTP** on port **80** from `0.0.0.0/0`

**Listeners & Routing:**
| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Protocol** | HTTP |
| **Port** | 80 |
| **Default action** | Forward to `WebApp-TG` |

4. Click **Create load balancer**

---

### Step 4: Create Auto Scaling Group

**AWS Console:**
1. Go to **EC2 → Auto Scaling Groups** → **Create Auto Scaling Group**
2. Fill in:

**Step 1: Choose launch template**
| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Name** | `WebApp-ASG` |
| **Launch template** | Select `WebApp-LT` |
| **Version** | Latest |

**Step 2: Configure settings**
| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **VPC** | Select default VPC |
| **Subnets** | Select **at least 2 AZs** |
| **Load balancing** | ✅ Attach to an existing load balancer |
| **Target group** | Select `WebApp-TG` |
| **Health checks** | ✅ Use ELB health checks |

**Step 3: Configure scaling policies**
| Setting | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Desired capacity** | `2` |
| **Minimum capacity** | `2` |
| **Maximum capacity** | `5` |

**Scaling Policy:**
1. Click **Add policy**
2. Choose **Target tracking scaling**
3. **Metric:** Average CPU utilization
4. **Target value:** `60`%
5. Click **Create**

---

### Step 5: Test Your Setup

**Get the Load Balancer DNS:**
1. Go to **EC2 → Load Balancers**
2. Select `WebApp-ALB`
3. Copy the **DNS name** (e.g., `webapp-alb-123456789.elb.amazonaws.com`)

**Open in browser:**
```
http://webapp-alb-123456789.elb.amazonaws.com
```

**Refresh multiple times** and watch the **Instance ID** change! 🎉

